/* ============================================================================
binomial-pmf.epher -- the binomial point probabilities, exact fractions

If X counts successes in n independent trials, each with success
probability p, then X ~ Bin(n, p) and

  P(X = k) = ncr(n, k) * p^k * (1 - p)^(n - k),

the classroom formula (Ross, "A First Course in Probability", ch. 4).
The script evaluates that formula with epher's exact ncr arithmetic
instead of the binompdf builtin, so the probabilities come out as
exact fractions, and binompdf is used afterwards only to cross-check
(the builtin prints floats with noise around the last digit, which the
comparison tolerates).

Cases: X ~ Bin(4, 1/3) (four independent trials, p = 1/3) shows the
full distribution as fractions, and X ~ Bin(10, 1/2) (ten fair coin
flips) shows the classic P(X = 3) = ncr(10, 3) / 2^10 = 15/128.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/distributions/binomial-pmf.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\distributions\binomial-pmf.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/distributions/binomial-pmf.epher
Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
============================================================================ */

// ---- helpers ----
def pmf(k, n, p) = ncr(n, k) * p ^ k * (1 - p) ^ (n - k)   // exact Bin(n, p) point probability

// ---- case 1: Bin(4, 1/3), the whole distribution as exact fractions ----
for k in 0 to 4 do pmf(k, 4, 1/3)

// The probabilities of a distribution sum to 1; the packed while loop
// (guide 1.30) adds the five exact fractions.
v = {0, 0}
while v[1] < 5 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + pmf(v[1], 4, 1/3)}
print("the five point probabilities sum to:", v[2])

// Cross-check one point against the binompdf builtin: the exact value
// is 8/27 = 0.296296296296..., and binompdf agrees to the noise level.
print("exact P(X = 2):", pmf(2, 4, 1/3))
print("binompdf(2, 4, 1/3):", binompdf(2, 4, 1/3))

// ---- case 2: Bin(10, 1/2), ten fair coin flips ----
// P(X = 3) = 120 / 1024 = 15/128 exactly; epher shows the terminating
// decimal 0.1171875.
print("exact P(X = 3):", pmf(3, 10, 1/2))
print("as a fraction: 15/128")
print("binompdf(3, 10, 0.5):", binompdf(3, 10, 0.5))
/* ---- expected output ----
= {16/81, 32/81, 8/27, 8/81, 1/81}
= {0, 0}
= the five point probabilities sum to: 1
= exact P(X = 2): 8/27
= binompdf(2, 4, 1/3): 0.296296296299
= exact P(X = 3): 0.1171875
= as a fraction: 15/128
= binompdf(3, 10, 0.5): 0.117187500002
*/
